SELECT 'How to Create PowerPoint Infographics with Microsoft Copilot', 'powerpoint-infographics-microsoft-copilot', CONCAT('Microsoft 365 Copilot can now turn a written brief or an uploaded document into a finished infographic', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' here is the exact click path, the prompt structure that produces a usable draft, and how to rebuild it natively in PowerPoint so it stays editable, on-brand and accessible.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> You no longer have to fight with shapes and alignment guides to build a decent infographic. The <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-copilot/create-an-infographic-with-the-microsoft-365-copilot-app">Microsoft 365 Copilot app now has a dedicated <strong>Design an infographic</strong> feature</a> that turns a written brief &mdash; or an uploaded document &mdash; into a finished visual you can download and drop straight into PowerPoint. This guide walks the exact click path, the prompt structure that actually produces a usable result, and how to finish the job properly in PowerPoint so the infographic is editable, on-brand and accessible. If you want it hands-on and funded, our <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-create-powerpoint-infographics-with-ai.html">CASL &ndash; Create Powerpoint Infographics with AI</a> course (course code TGS-2026064178) covers it in one day.</p> <h2>Why infographics stall in most teams</h2> <p>Almost every workplace deck has the same weak slide: a wall of bullet points that is really a process, a comparison or a set of numbers wearing a text costume. Everyone knows a visual would land better. It still does not get made, and the reason is rarely a lack of taste &mdash; it is the cost of the first draft.</p> <p>Building an infographic from scratch in PowerPoint means choosing a structure, laying out shapes, sourcing icons, aligning everything, picking a colour scheme that does not clash with the corporate template, and then discovering that the content does not quite fit the layout you committed to twenty minutes ago. For a slide that may get four seconds of attention, that is a hard cost to justify at 6pm.</p> <p>What AI changes is not the craft &mdash; it is that first draft. Getting to a structured, laid-out starting point in under a minute moves the work from <em>creation</em> to <em>editing</em>, and editing is a job most professionals can do well and quickly. That is the entire productivity argument, and it is worth being precise about it, because the failure mode is expecting the tool to deliver a finished, on-brand, factually checked asset with no human pass. It will not.</p> <h2>What you need before you start</h2> <p>Microsoft is explicit about the prerequisites, and they are worth checking before you promise anyone a deliverable:</p> <ul> <li><strong>An eligible Microsoft 365 subscription.</strong> The infographic feature lives in the Microsoft 365 Copilot app. Personal, Family and Premium subscribers can use it but face <strong>usage limits</strong>.</li> <li><strong>Licensing affects quality.</strong> Microsoft notes that users without a Microsoft Copilot license get variable results, and that &ldquo;if the service isn&rsquo;t available, Copilot notifies you.&rdquo; If your organisation has not assigned you a licence, that is the first thing to resolve.</li> <li><strong>Your content, ready to paste or upload.</strong> The single biggest quality lever is having your actual numbers, steps or comparison points to hand &mdash; not asking the model to invent them. More on that below, because it matters more than any prompt trick.</li> <li><strong>PowerPoint, for the finishing pass.</strong> The generated image is the draft; the editable, brand-correct, accessible version is built in PowerPoint.</li> </ul> <h2>Step 1: Generate the infographic in the Microsoft 365 Copilot app</h2> <p>The click path is short and, per <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-copilot/create-an-infographic-with-the-microsoft-365-copilot-app">Microsoft&rsquo;s own documentation</a>, goes like this:</p> <ol> <li>Open the <strong>Microsoft 365 Copilot</strong> app and select <strong>Create</strong> from the left sidebar.</li> <li>Choose <strong>Design an infographic</strong>. If you do not see it in the list, select <strong>More...</strong> to find it.</li> <li>Select <strong>Describe your infographic</strong> and type your brief. Microsoft&rsquo;s own guidance is blunt about what works: <em>&ldquo;The more detail you offer, the better your results will tend to be.&rdquo;</em></li> <li>Optionally select <strong>+Add content</strong> to upload a file &mdash; a report, a spreadsheet, a set of meeting notes &mdash; and have the infographic built from that source rather than from your typing.</li> <li>Select <strong>Brand and color</strong> to apply your organisation&rsquo;s brand kit, if your tenant has one configured.</li> <li>Pick a format: <strong>Portrait</strong> or <strong>Wide</strong>. For a PowerPoint slide, choose <strong>Wide</strong> &mdash; portrait will letterbox awkwardly on a 16:9 slide and you will lose real estate on both sides.</li> <li>Select <strong>Create</strong>.</li> </ol> <p>Once it generates, you have three options: <strong>Copy</strong>, <strong>Download</strong>, or <strong>Edit</strong> &mdash; the last of which opens the result in an editor where you can add text, apply filters and make further changes. Everything you make is saved to the <strong>Copilot Library</strong>, which is where to look when you cannot find yesterday&rsquo;s version.</p> <h2>Step 2: Write a brief that produces a usable draft</h2> <p>&ldquo;Make an infographic about our sales performance&rdquo; produces exactly what you would expect: something generic, with invented-looking numbers and no point of view. A brief that works specifies five things, and it is worth keeping this as a checklist:</p> <table> <thead> <tr><th>Element</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Example</th></tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr><td><strong>The content itself</strong></td><td>Supply your real figures and labels. Do not leave the model to fill gaps &mdash; that is where fabricated numbers come from</td><td>&ldquo;Q1 42%, Q2 51%, Q3 47%, Q4 63%&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>The structure</strong></td><td>Names the visual pattern so the layout matches the logic of your content</td><td>&ldquo;a 5-step horizontal process flow&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>The audience</strong></td><td>Drives vocabulary and how much explanation each element needs</td><td>&ldquo;for non-technical senior management&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>The takeaway</strong></td><td>Gives the design something to emphasise, instead of weighting everything equally</td><td>&ldquo;the key message is that Q4 recovered above target&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td><strong>The style and format</strong></td><td>Controls tone and fit before you waste a regeneration</td><td>&ldquo;clean corporate style, minimal colours, wide format&rdquo;</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Put together, a brief that reliably produces something worth editing reads more like this:</p> <pre>Create a wide-format infographic showing our 5-step customer onboarding process:&#10;1. Enquiry received  2. Needs assessment  3. Proposal sent&#10;4. Contract signed  5. Kickoff session&#10;&#10;Audience: non-technical senior management.&#10;Key message: the process now takes 12 days, down from 21.&#10;Style: clean corporate, minimal colour palette, plenty of white space.</pre> <p>Two habits separate people who get good results from people who conclude &ldquo;AI infographics do not work&rdquo;. First, <strong>iterate rather than restart</strong> &mdash; ask for a change to what you have rather than rewriting the prompt from scratch and regenerating something entirely different. Second, <strong>match the structure to the content</strong>. A timeline for sequential events, a comparison layout for options, a statistical layout for figures, a process flow for steps. If you ask for &ldquo;an infographic&rdquo; without naming the pattern, you get whatever the model guesses.</p> <h2>Step 3: Check the facts before the design</h2> <p>This step is short, comes before any polishing, and is the one most likely to be skipped.</p> <p>An AI-generated infographic can render a number that never existed in your source, round something in a way that changes its meaning, or label an axis plausibly but wrongly. The design quality of the output actively works against you here &mdash; a clean, confident-looking visual gets challenged far less than an ugly one. Read every figure, label and percentage against your source data before you spend a second on colours.</p> <p>The same applies to claims. If the generated visual has added a supporting statement you did not supply, either substantiate it or delete it. You own what goes in the deck, regardless of what produced the pixels.</p> <h2>Step 4: Bring it into PowerPoint and make it editable</h2> <p>A downloaded infographic is a flat image. That is fine for a one-off, but it has real limitations: you cannot fix a typo, the text will not scale cleanly on a projector, screen readers cannot reach the content, and it will not follow your template when branding changes. For anything that will be reused, rebuild the key structure natively.</p> <p>The practical workflow most people land on is a hybrid one:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Insert the generated image</strong> on a blank slide as your reference layout.</li> <li><strong>Rebuild the structural elements natively</strong> &mdash; use <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/create-a-smartart-graphic-fac94c93-500b-4a0a-97af-124040594842">SmartArt</a> for process flows, cycles and hierarchies, native <strong>Charts</strong> for anything data-driven (so the numbers stay linked and updatable), and <strong>Icons</strong> from <strong>Insert &gt; Icons</strong> for the visual anchors.</li> <li><strong>Apply your theme colours</strong> rather than eyedropping the generated palette. Using theme colours means the infographic re-skins automatically if your corporate template changes.</li> <li><strong>Set the type properly.</strong> Nothing below 14pt on a slide that will be projected. If your infographic needs 10pt text to fit, it has too much content, and shrinking the font is the wrong fix.</li> <li><strong>Delete the reference image</strong> once the native version stands on its own.</li> </ol> <p>Worth knowing: <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/create-a-presentation-with-copilot-in-powerpoint-3222ee03-f5a4-4d27-8642-9c387ab4854d">Copilot inside PowerPoint</a> is a separate capability from the infographic generator &mdash; it builds and restructures whole presentations. The two work well together: generate the visual concept in the Copilot app, then use Copilot in PowerPoint to help assemble the surrounding deck.</p> <h2>Step 5: Make it readable &mdash; the design rules AI will not enforce</h2> <p>Copilot handles the layout. It does not know your room, your audience, or your accessibility obligations. Five checks catch most problems:</p> <ul> <li><strong>One message per infographic.</strong> If you cannot state the single takeaway in a sentence, the graphic is doing two jobs and should be two graphics.</li> <li><strong>Visual hierarchy that matches importance.</strong> The most important number should be the largest thing on the slide. If everything is the same size, you have made a diagram, not an infographic.</li> <li><strong>Contrast that survives a projector.</strong> Aim for a <strong>4.5:1</strong> contrast ratio for body text, per <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/quickref/#contrast-minimum">WCAG 2.1</a>. Light grey text on white looks elegant on your laptop and disappears in a bright meeting room.</li> <li><strong>Never encode meaning in colour alone.</strong> Around 1 in 12 men has some form of colour vision deficiency. Pair colour with a label, an icon or a pattern so a red/green distinction is not the only signal.</li> <li><strong>Add alt text.</strong> Right-click the graphic, choose <strong>View Alt Text</strong>, and describe what it communicates &mdash; not &ldquo;infographic&rdquo;, but the actual message. This is what a screen reader announces, and it is a legal requirement in many contexts.</li> </ul> <p>One more, learned the hard way by anyone who has presented remotely: check the slide at <strong>50% zoom</strong> before you finish. If it stops being readable, it will not survive a Teams call on a laptop screen.</p> <h2>Which infographic type fits your content</h2> <p>Choosing the pattern before you write the prompt saves the most time. The common workplace cases:</p> <table> <thead> <tr><th>Your content</th><th>Use this type</th><th>Ask Copilot for</th></tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Sequential steps</td><td>Process flow</td><td>&ldquo;a horizontal 5-step process flow with numbered stages&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td>Events over time</td><td>Timeline</td><td>&ldquo;a horizontal timeline with 6 milestones and dates&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td>Options against criteria</td><td>Comparison</td><td>&ldquo;a side-by-side comparison of three options across four criteria&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td>Headline figures</td><td>Statistical</td><td>&ldquo;a statistical infographic with four large key figures and short labels&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td>Reporting lines</td><td>Organisational chart</td><td>&ldquo;an organisation chart with three levels&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td>Components of a whole</td><td>Hierarchy or breakdown</td><td>&ldquo;a hierarchy diagram breaking one category into five sub-categories&rdquo;</td></tr> <tr><td>Several metrics at once</td><td>Dashboard</td><td>&ldquo;a business dashboard layout with four KPI tiles and one trend chart&rdquo;</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <h2>A 20-minute workflow you can use tomorrow</h2> <ol> <li><strong>Minutes 0&ndash;3 &mdash; decide the message.</strong> Write the one-sentence takeaway and gather the real numbers. Skipping this is why most attempts stall.</li> <li><strong>Minutes 3&ndash;5 &mdash; write the brief.</strong> Content, structure, audience, takeaway, style. Use <strong>+Add content</strong> if the source is a document you already have.</li> <li><strong>Minutes 5&ndash;10 &mdash; generate and iterate.</strong> Two or three refinements, asking for changes rather than regenerating from scratch.</li> <li><strong>Minutes 10&ndash;12 &mdash; fact-check.</strong> Every figure and label against the source. Non-negotiable.</li> <li><strong>Minutes 12&ndash;18 &mdash; rebuild in PowerPoint.</strong> SmartArt, native charts, theme colours, proper type sizes.</li> <li><strong>Minutes 18&ndash;20 &mdash; accessibility pass.</strong> Contrast, alt text, and the 50% zoom check.</li> </ol> <p>Six of those twenty minutes are the AI. The other fourteen are judgment &mdash; which is exactly the split you want, and a fair description of what these tools actually do to knowledge work.</p> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>Reading a click path and building a boardroom-ready infographic under time pressure are different things. Our <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-create-powerpoint-infographics-with-ai.html">CASL &ndash; Create Powerpoint Infographics with AI</a> course (course code <strong>TGS-2026064178</strong>) is a <strong>1-day, 8-hour</strong> beginner-level programme that takes you through the whole workflow with your own material, covering:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Topic 1:</strong> AI-Assisted Infographic Planning and Data Insights</li> <li><strong>Topic 2:</strong> Creating PowerPoint Infographics with AI</li> <li><strong>Topic 3:</strong> Evaluating and Presenting Infographics for Business Impact</li> </ul> <p>It maps to the Skills Framework TSC <strong>Infographics and Data Visualisation</strong> (TOU-DAT-4004-1.1-1), and you finish with a Certificate of Achievement on meeting the attendance and assessment requirements.</p> <p>The fee is <strong>$350 before GST</strong>. With funding, Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above pay a <strong>nett $206.50</strong> (50% funded); Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES) and eligible SMEs pay a <strong>nett $136.50</strong> (70% funded). You can offset the balance with <strong>SkillsFuture Credit</strong>, and eligible Singapore-registered companies can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> of up to $10,000. Classes run at Woods Square (5 minutes from Woodlands MRT), as synchronous online sessions over Zoom, or on your company premises &mdash; with weekday, weekend and evening slots.</p> <p>If your work sits alongside this, these run on the same Microsoft Copilot foundation:</p> <ul> <li><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-enhance-work-productivity-with-microsoft-365-copilot.html">WSQ &ndash; Enhance Work Productivity with Microsoft 365 Copilot</a> &mdash; the broader Copilot workflow across Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams.</li> <li><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-for-business-presentations.html">WSQ &ndash; Generative AI for Business Presentations</a> &mdash; building the whole deck, not just the visual.</li> <li><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/microsoft-365-copilot-masterclass.html">Microsoft 365 Copilot Masterclass</a> &mdash; the non-WSQ intensive if you want the tool itself first.</li> <li><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/visual-storytelling-with-powerpoint.html">Visual Storytelling with PowerPoint</a> &mdash; the design and narrative craft behind the slide.</li> <li>Browse the full <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/microsoft-copilot-series.html">Microsoft Copilot Series</a> and <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/infographics-courses.html">Infographics courses</a>.</li> </ul> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need a paid Microsoft 365 Copilot licence to create infographics?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>You need an eligible Microsoft 365 subscription to use the <strong>Design an infographic</strong> feature in the Microsoft 365 Copilot app. Personal, Family and Premium subscribers can use it but are subject to usage limits. Microsoft also notes that users without a Microsoft Copilot licence may see variable quality, and that Copilot will notify you if the service is not available to your account.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Can Copilot build an infographic from a document I already have?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. In the infographic creator, select <strong>+Add content</strong> to upload a file and have the infographic generated from that source. This is usually better than typing a summary yourself, because the figures come from your actual document rather than from a paraphrase &mdash; though you should still fact-check the rendered numbers against the source.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does the infographic come into PowerPoint as editable shapes?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No &mdash; you download it as an image, so the text and shapes are not individually editable in PowerPoint. For a one-off that is fine. For anything reused or updated, use the generated image as a reference layout and rebuild the key structure natively with SmartArt, charts and icons, so the content stays editable, accessible to screen readers, and correct when your branding changes.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Why does my infographic come out generic?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Almost always because the brief was thin. Supply your real content, name the structure you want (process flow, timeline, comparison, statistical), state the audience, state the single takeaway, and specify the style and format. Then refine what you get rather than regenerating from a rewritten prompt &mdash; iterating on an existing draft keeps the parts that already worked.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is it safe to trust the numbers Copilot puts in the graphic?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Verify every one. Generative AI can render figures that were never in your source, or round in a way that changes the meaning. The risk is higher than usual with infographics because a polished visual invites less scrutiny than a rough one. Check each figure and label against your data before you do any design work &mdash; you own what appears in the deck.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Portrait or Wide format?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p><strong>Wide</strong> for anything going on a PowerPoint slide, since standard slides are 16:9 and a portrait graphic wastes space on both sides. <strong>Portrait</strong> suits a printed handout, a one-pager, or a social or mobile-first post where people scroll vertically.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is the CASL Create Powerpoint Infographics with AI course funded, and what will I pay?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. The fee is $350 before GST. Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above pay a nett $206.50 (50% funded); Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES) and eligible SMEs pay a nett $136.50 (70% funded). You can offset the balance with SkillsFuture Credit, and eligible companies can tap SFEC of up to $10,000. It runs 1 day (8 hours) with a 1-hour assessment, and requires no prior design background.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need design experience to attend?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. The course is pitched at beginner level, and the entry requirement is basic computer literacy plus around a year of working experience &mdash; not a design background. The design principles you need (hierarchy, layout, colour, typography, storytelling) are taught as part of the course. Bring your own laptop, or use one of the spare units in the classroom.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>Microsoft 365 Copilot removes the part of infographic work that was blocking most people &mdash; the blank canvas and the first forty minutes of shape-pushing. What it does not remove is the thinking: deciding the message, verifying the numbers, choosing the right visual pattern, and making the result readable for everyone in the room. Get that division of labour right and a decent infographic stops being a Friday-evening project and becomes a twenty-minute task.</p> <p>Ready to build them properly? <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-create-powerpoint-infographics-with-ai.html">Register for CASL &ndash; Create Powerpoint Infographics with AI</a> &mdash; 1 day, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
